# Reviewer 3 — Boolean decomposition/logic synthesis perspective

Same-assistant specialist perspective; not external peer review.

**Recommendation: no new decomposition-paper claim.** XOR, products, cofactor dictionaries, Shannon decomposition and GF(2) factorization have substantial antecedents. A 16-cut bounded search selecting serialized size is not global optimality, and its output cannot establish superiority to established decomposition techniques without comparable search budgets.

The inner-product example is a legitimate fixed-cut dictionary comparison but an especially poor all-portfolio lower-bound example: disjoint pairwise products immediately expose a compact XOR decomposition. Blocked and interleaved orders differ dramatically. An implementation should record the chosen cut and charge its search rather than selecting favorable cuts retrospectively.

I support retaining the new corpus screen as transparent preparation. It uses original designs, accounts for all outputs and separates families. Still, many large-support outputs are excluded; two admitted cuts do not characterize best possible decomposition. The fixed support cap must remain in every incidence statement.

I disagree with a reading of the systems report that a credible counting comparator alone would clear decomposition novelty. It clears the count endpoint comparison only. If decomposition quality is later claimed, add an established decomposition method with equalized order/cut/search accounting. LUT delay optimization is not conditioned-count runtime. **SEND RESULTS/QUESTION TO THEORY** about the actual claim before expanding comparators.
